WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:14.040
Today’s lesson goal: Today’s lesson goal: treat A I work as engineering, not magic. You will name what models do well and where they fail. You will leave with a spec and evaluation habit you can reuse.

2
00:00:14.890 --> 00:00:27.466
What an LLM actually does: An L L M, or language model, predicts likely next tokens from patterns. It can sound confident even when guessing. Your constraints keep it useful.

3
00:00:28.316 --> 00:00:41.828
Strengths you can trust more: Strengths you can trust more: drafting and rewriting text you will review. Summarising meeting notes into action lists. Transforming content into a format you define.

4
00:00:42.678 --> 00:00:58.374
Failure modes to watch: Watch for failure modes. Hallucinations invent facts or owners. Brittleness means tiny wording changes break output. Injection risk means untrusted text tries to override your rules.

5
00:00:59.224 --> 00:01:14.872
Worked example: support ticket: Worked example: a support ticket. Goal: a summary and next steps from the ticket. Constraint: only use the provided text. Failure to avoid: inventing a refund promise.

6
00:01:15.722 --> 00:01:31.370
The engineering loop: Use the engineering loop: spec, prompt, evaluate, iterate. Write the goal, inputs, outputs, and failure modes. Keep a ten-case evaluation set and rerun it after each change.

7
00:01:32.220 --> 00:01:51.060
Practice before you finish: Practice before you finish. Copy the beginner starter pack templates. Write a one-page summariser spec. Build ten evaluation cases for good, bad, and ambiguous inputs. Then continue in the written Module 1 lesson.
